<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <GeneratePackageOnBuild>True</GeneratePackageOnBuild>
    <Title>EasyReasy.Auth.Microsoft</Title>
    <Authors>Adam Tovatt</Authors>
    <Description>Sign in with Microsoft work and school accounts for EasyReasy.Auth, through the server-side authorization code flow with PKCE, with per-request tenant, freshness and Conditional Access authentication context checks</Description>
    <Copyright>MIT</Copyright>
    <PackageProjectUrl>https://github.com/AdamTovatt/easy-reasy</PackageProjectUrl>
    <PackageReadmeFile>README.md</PackageReadmeFile>
    <RepositoryUrl>https://github.com/AdamTovatt/easy-reasy</RepositoryUrl>
    <PackageTags>auth;jwt;microsoft;entra;azuread;oidc;signin;easyreasy</PackageTags>
    <PackageLicenseExpression>MIT</PackageLicenseExpression>
    <IncludeSymbols>True</IncludeSymbols>
    <SymbolPackageFormat>snupkg</SymbolPackageFormat>
    <GenerateDocumentationFile>True</GenerateDocumentationFile>
    <VersionPrefix>2.0.0</VersionPrefix>
    <VersionSuffix>preview.1</VersionSuffix>
    <PackageIcon>BaseIcon.png</PackageIcon>
    <PackageReleaseNotes>Rebuilt on EasyReasy.Auth.OpenIdConnect's shared code flow, keeping the Entra rules and the tid plus oid identity. Breaking: Begin is BeginAsync and returns OpenIdConnectAuthBeginResult; CompleteAsync and POST /api/auth/microsoft take an OpenIdConnectAuthorizationResponse, which adds the iss parameter and the redirectUri the response arrived at, and a response at another redirect URI than its flow named is refused; CompleteAsync takes the expected caller binding and refuses a flow begun with another as CallerBindingMismatch before redeeming its code, so the result no longer carries CallerBinding; prompts are OpenIdConnectPrompt; HttpClientName is OpenIdConnectCodeFlow.HttpClientName; four failure reasons are added. Behaviour: Begin fetches Microsoft's OpenID configuration, so it calls the network and throws while Microsoft is unreachable; a refusal of the client itself (invalid_client, such as an expired secret) throws instead of returning CodeRedemptionFailed; on a flow that named a tenant, a token issued by another tenant is InvalidIssuer with no account, where it was TenantMismatch with the account. A preview until a security review. Requires EasyReasy.Auth 5.19.0 and EasyReasy.Auth.OpenIdConnect 1.0.0-preview.1. See https://github.com/AdamTovatt/easy-reasy/blob/auth-microsoft-v$(VersionPrefix)-$(VersionSuffix)/EasyReasy.Auth.Microsoft/README.md</PackageReleaseNotes>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <None Include="..\Icons\PNG\Normal\128w\BaseIcon.png">
      <Pack>True</Pack>
      <PackagePath>\</PackagePath>
    </None>
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="EasyReasy.Auth.Microsoft.Tests" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <FrameworkReference Include="Microsoft.AspNetCore.App" />
    <!-- No IdentityModel reference of its own: EasyReasy.Auth brings the whole family at one version, which
         IdentityModelVersionAlignmentTests, linked into the test project, guards. -->
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\EasyReasy.Auth\EasyReasy.Auth.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\EasyReasy.Auth.OpenIdConnect\EasyReasy.Auth.OpenIdConnect.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <None Update="README.md">
      <Pack>True</Pack>
      <PackagePath>\</PackagePath>
    </None>
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
